A||||22||Cliff Pennock||Slick 50 - Opinions?||||||
Z||000000||Cliff Pennock||06-03-99||05:39 PM||||When I got my first Fiero back in '89 one of the first things I did when I changed the oil was to add Slick 50. It <i>did</i> idle much smoother after that and it improved mileage a little bit. Two years after that, when I thought it would be time to change the oil again, the guy asked me if I had changed the oil just recently because it was still as clear as new oil.<p>After that, I've always put Slick 50 in every other car I owned and have always been <b>very</b> content with it. Does anyone else have any experience with Slick 50? What is your opinion about this and similar products?
Z||000001||Motorhead||06-03-99||06:45 PM||||I use it as assembly lubricant. No complaints, but synthetic oils really don't need any additives.<p>A good synthetic will come out at oil change time looking like the day you put it in. In my opinion forget the "snake oil" additives and use a good synthetic oil to start with!  <p>The extra cost will more than equal out in engine life!
Z||000002||Bruce||06-04-99||12:45 PM||||Have you ever seen "King of the Hill." The father of the featured family once said: "I change my oil every 3,000 miles or whenever I get bored." Words to live by?
Z||000003||theogre||06-04-99||03:25 PM||||I tend to not trust any of that stuff to much. None of it ever seemed to cause a problem, but none of it ever realy seemded to do much good either.<p>Slick 50 used to be a great on for that run a motor of some type w/o oil.... a demo we all know is basically crap. I thought it was pretty funny when consumer reports ( i think) tried the drive a car w/o oil stunt and only got couple miles before the motor let go. 
Z||000004||FieroMike||06-04-99||04:46 PM||||I would have to stick with Ogre on this one.  My grandpa was a GM Engineer and he said the best thing for a motor, is motor oil.  I think Ogre is talking about the famous "ProLong" with that Viper.  I don't think I'd try that with my viper.
Z||000005||Patrick||06-04-99||07:19 PM||||<br>I also treat with scepticism anything I hear or read about oil additives. However, having said that, here's my little story. <p>My other car, other than the Fiero of course, is a ‘78 Z28 Camaro. I decided to try using <b>Dura-Lube</b> in the crankcase, and also in the 4-speed manual transmission. (It's a differently packaged container for transmissions.)<p>I'll say right now that I can NOT vouch for the effectiveness of Dura-Lube in the engine. But I do have something to say about this additive in my transmission. <p>Unknown to me at the time, the rear seal of my transmission had failed and I was losing gear oil (and Dura-Lube) out the back of the transmission. It was a slow process, but eventually the level got lower and lower. I noticed that the transmission was getting noisier and noisier. I attributed that to the fact that there were a lot of hard miles of this car, due to the fact that the engine is not stock and it puts out quite a few ponies. Transmission failure is to be expected eventually, and I thought that nothing "out of the ordinary" was happening. However, over the course of a couple of months, the noise got to the point where I was embarrassed to drive the car. It sounded like the tranny was full of mismatched gears that were all fighting for dominance within the confined space of a Borg Warner Super T-10. I reluctantly decided I had to pull the tranny.<p>Well, it didn't take me very long to realize what had happened when I got under the car. The bottom of the car was covered in oil, from the rear of the transmission back. As if that wasn't enough of an indication of what had been going on, when I removed the drain plug, nothing, I repeat NOTHING drained out of the transmission. I slapped myself a few times for being so stupid, and then proceeded to pull the transmission out. I remember having visions of destroyed bearings and trashed gears.<p>When I tore down the transmission, I could find NO problem with any of the bearings or the gears. All they needed was some oil because they were bone dry. There was no more "powdered" metal in the bottom of the tranny than you'd find in any transmission that was in good shape. I put her back together with nothing more than a new rear seal and new gaskets, and she worked like a charm. No more noisier than any other Borg Warner Super T-10.<p>I can only attribute this to the fact that something in the Dura-Lube did indeed <b>bond</b> to all the metal parts in the transmission. I shudder to think what would have happened if I had run the transmission this dry and driven it for as long as I did without some kind of an oil additive. Whether or not Dura-Lube is any better than the other additives, I couldn't say.<p>I do NOT have a vested interest in Dura-Lube, but I am a satisfied customer. Short of drinking the stuff, I use it for almost everything. You can bet my Fiero has tasted it. <IMG SRC="http://forums.gagames.com/forums/smile.gif"> <p><br> 
Z||000006||Gokart||06-04-99||07:53 PM||||Was thinking about synthetic oil for my GT, but was told that once you have over 100,000 you should stick with regular oil. Synthetic's I was told should only be used at the start or not at all. What have you all heard on this topic?
Z||000007||86_Fastback||06-04-99||09:10 PM||||I bought my 86 GT in January with over 110,000 miles.  First thing I did was change the oil to synthetic.  It really freed the engine up.  The only real item to think about is the shape of your seals and gaskets.  With synthetic, it will clean out sludge and deposits that are preventing bad seals to leak.  If you want to try synthetic, I recomend Mobil 1.  It is readily available and is what GM puts in new Corvettes and LS1 f-bodies.<br>-Gregg
Z||000008||Racer 1||06-04-99||11:24 PM||||I put that Pro-Long and slick 50 stuff in my engine and didnt notice a thing.Now I use to use regular quaker state oil and when I would go up in the mountains I would notice in my rear view mirror white smoke coming out my drivers side rear vent.I have a remote crankcase breather which sits under the drivers rear grill.But when I switched to Mobil 1 synthetic oil the white smoke was never present when going up the same mountain road!Mobil one is expensive but well worth the $$$$! I also heard slick 50 had some kinda chemical patf or something like that word that harmed your engine!Has anyone heard of this problem with slick 50?
Z||000009||Harry||06-06-99||06:53 PM||||Im not going to blow smoke out of your tail pipes.<br>1973 I wittnessed the famed test running the engine dry with slick 50 in my auto shop class. I also read where slick 50 was investigated by Wash. for there claim and passed with flying colors.<br>While I am not a spokesman nor affilated with said company I HAVE USED slick 50 in many cars over the last 20+ years and have even torn down engines after several (50K) plus miles on them.<br>slick 50 has never left the tell tale carbon build up in any crankcase that I have used it in (including the nasty EGR motors)<br>I firmly believe in the "if its dark and dirty its history" oil change policy.<br>My choice of engine oil for the last ten years or so has been Castrol.<br>For an engine with alot of miles, well PTFE resin in slick 50 is well worth the money and considering when it first came out it was 50$ plus dollars a quart its alot cheaper these days.<br>Mobil 1 is the best thing for any engine (synthetic) however if you have even one small leak, stand by for major oil loss after changing to synthetics because if it goes in like water you can bet your engine it will come out just as fast!!!!
Z||000010||ggarsk||08-27-99||07:56 PM||||I put Slick 50 in a 351M/400. At idle it carried no oil pressure and only 20 lbs at 55 mph. I drove that motor for 3 years and when I pulled it out it still ran. if your wondering why I pulled it,I dropped a 429 in my pickup.
Z||000011||theogre||08-27-99||08:12 PM||||for the giggles I threw in some of the duralube..... one of those 15$ promo pak things.....<p>Didn't hurt anything, but didn't seem to make any more dif than any other oil changes. I figure with 136K on it any little bit was worth a try.<p>Tried some trans-X in my dogde cravan a couple weeks ago.... helped a bit, but still shifts 1-2 shitty at low RPM.
Z||000012||Dave85||08-27-99||08:47 PM||||I just switched to synthetic a couple of weeks ago and threw in a bottle of Prolong. Well you're right abut the oil leaks! My car does run alot smoother. The biggest differance is in the cooling dept. My car runs MUCH cooler now.
Z||000013||fierofast||08-27-99||09:14 PM||||I have heard and seen better results from a product called ER which stands for energy release.  We used a friction brake machine to test the oils.  This test also shows wear damage on an actual bearing.  Slick 50 also did good but not as good.
Z||000014||batboy||08-27-99||10:58 PM||||I used Slick 50 in one of my rebuilt engines once as a test and didn't really notice much difference. I recently ran across an article about Slick 50 and some other additives, check out the below listed web address:<p>http://www.jps.net/gtracer/snakeoil.htm
Z||000015||DJRice||08-30-99||07:44 AM||||Well, I used the Slick 50 in my Buick 5.0L and after 160k miles the timing chain broke.<p>Coincidence? I think not.......<p>&lt;joking&gt; <IMG SRC="/ubb/smile.gif" WIDTH=15 HEIGHT=15>
Z||000016||stsmithgt||02-03-2000||12:34 PM||||The first thing I did when I bought my 86GT was to put in Prolong in the engine and tranny.  I was lucky, the previous owner took good care of this car.  I also kept up with the prolong booster offered for each oil change.  Since then I've added Duralube and quit the Prolong, I run Castrol synthetic 5w-50w.  I was changing the oil every 2,000 miles for the first year I had it.  Now at every 3,000.  At 109,000 miles,  the engine sounds loose to me, but my ears here every rattle and bump the engine makes.  A mechanic I've used in Sacramento CA. says it's one of the best running ones he's seen.  I do all this stuff because I fear the price of replacing the engine and the question of rebuilding the tranny while it's out(4spd).  I"m glad other folks have good things to say about these types of additives.||||reg||10
Z||000017||mrfixit58||02-03-2000||04:56 PM||||I tried the Slick 50 in my 88 4.3 S-10 Jimmy. The truck had about 30,000 miles on it when I added it with an oil change. At my next oil change I pulled the drain plug and let it sit as usual while I did something else for a little while. When I got back to the oil change, I noticed something hanging out of the drain hole, kinda thread like. I pulled it out and dobbed the oil off. It was white and looked just like teflon tape about three inches long. <P>The motor continued to run great up to 98,000 miles (that's when it was stoled and burned to the ground). The only negative I've heard is the teflon particulates can clog an oil filter and cause oil starvation. Unless you driving a Viper, a motor will die if starved of oil.<P>I agree with many of the previous statements that, if you going to spend extra, spend it on Moble 1.<P>Just my $0.02,<BR>Roy L.||||reg||1
Z||000018||batboy||02-03-2000||09:57 PM||||I think these additives are mostly "snake oil" gimmicks. Check out this article.<P> <A HREF="http://www.jps.net/gtracer/snakeoil.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jps.net/gtracer/snakeoil.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000019||fieroshop||02-04-2000||08:36 PM||||Just my .02 cents worth. I don't believe in slick 50 or any other additives. When I install a fresh engine I use castorl for the first 300 miles and then switch to red line. That is what I recommend to all my customers in my shop and have not regretted it for one moment. The reason you don't use synthetics right off the bat is that the engine will not seat and you will costantly burn oil.||||reg||7
Z||000020||Formula||02-04-2000||08:36 PM||||I thought I was the only one who noticed that oil with dialkyldithiophosphate was a little bit better. hmmmmmmmm.......<P>I would put syntehtic in my car but it already urinates oil, it drops down on top of the auto tranny and then onto the ground, and ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000021||Patrick||02-04-2000||09:14 PM||||<BR>The oil won't drip on the tranny if you turn the car over.||||reg||1
Z||000022||Formula||02-04-2000||09:36 PM||||I never thought of that.<P>||||reg||1
